9.3  Copy-on-write filesystems: snapshots and checksums

TL;DR
  - Btrfs and ZFS never overwrite data in place: changes go to new blocks, then
    one pointer switches. The filesystem is always consistent.
  - That makes snapshots almost free: a snapshot keeps the old pointers, and
    both share every unchanged block.
  - They checksum data as well as metadata, so silent corruption (bit rot) is
    detected, and repaired if there's a second copy.

      sudo btrfs subvolume snapshot / /.snapshots/before-upgrade
      sudo btrfs scrub start /         # read everything, verify checksums
      cp --reflink=always big.img copy.img   # instant copy, shares blocks

On your machine
  /         btrfs on /dev/nvme0n1p2 · SSD mode
  /home     btrfs on /dev/nvme0n1p2 · SSD mode
  /var/lib/my data btrfs on /dev/nvme0n1p2 · SSD mode
  /boot/efi vfat on /dev/nvme0n1p1
  /media/usb exfat on /dev/sda
  /home     btrfs on /dev/nvme0n1p2

Pitfalls
  - A snapshot on the same disk isn't a backup: if the disk dies, both go.
  - Databases and VM images, rewritten in place constantly, fragment badly under
    copy-on-write. chattr +C on their empty folder turns it off, and with it
    checksums and compression for those files.
  - Old snapshots keep deleted data alive: free space comes back only when
    they're deleted.
  - ZFS isn't in the Linux kernel (license): it comes as a separate module,
    which must be rebuilt for each new kernel.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with atfq 9.3 --why

